{"boundary":"Exhaustive counts, not estimates; the derivation is a proof for every odd p^n and uses only that 2 is a unit.  The orbit count BOUNDS the characteristic-polynomial image (Pass 537) but does not equal it: merging occurs (Pass 540 exhibited a q=5 full-support cospectral pair), so the spectral image is strictly smaller and is not addressed here.","checks":{"counts_are_integers":true,"field_and_ring_both_covered":true,"formula_reproduces_every_known_count":true,"new_values_are_integers":true,"new_values_are_z25_and_z27":true,"structural_formulas_verified":true},"headline":"ONE SIGNED-CYCLE BURNSIDE FORMULA FOR EVERY ODD Z/p^n.  Over R = Z/p^n with p odd, SL(2,R) permutes the (p^{2n}-1)/2 antipodal pairs with a sign; a negative cycle forces the section to zero since 2 is a unit and a positive cycle is free, so |Fix_all(g)| = (p^n)^{c+(g)} and |Fix_full(g)| = (p^n-1)^{c+(g)} with no negative cycle else 0, and Burnside averages over |SL(2,R)| = p^{3n-2}(p^2-1).  This reproduces every count already computed -- the field values 7 and 2,034,735 (Pass 537), the full-support 2 and 139,904 (Passes 539/540), and BOTH exact Z/9 chain-ring integers (Pass 540) -- so Pass 537 (n=1) and Pass 540 ((p,n)=(3,2)) are one formula.  Z/25 and Z/27 are produced here exactly for the first time.","part_A_certification":{"known_sources":{"Z/3":"Pass 537 (all) and Pass 539 (full support = 2)","Z/5":"Pass 537 (all) and Pass 540 (full support)","Z/9":"Pass 540 (both, exact chain-ring integers)"},"reading":"The single signed-cycle average reproduces all five previously computed counts to the last digit -- three field values from Pass 537/539 and both exact Z/9 chain-ring integers from Pass 540.  The field cases are n = 1 and Z/9 is (p,n) = (3,2), so Pass 537 and Pass 540 are one formula.","rows":{"Z/3":{"all_orbits":"7","antipodal_pairs":4,"full_support_orbits":"2","matches_corpus":true,"sl_order":24},"Z/5":{"all_orbits":"2034735","antipodal_pairs":12,"full_support_orbits":"139904","matches_corpus":true,"sl_order":120},"Z/9":{"all_orbits":"228100045392509153077600971330057241","antipodal_pairs":40,"full_support_orbits":"2051277771273019233341050472890368","matches_corpus":true,"sl_order":648}}},"part_B_new_values":{"reading":"Z/25 and Z/27 are the next two odd prime-power rings after Z/9.  Their orbit counts on 25^312 and 27^364 sections respectively are produced here for the first time, all-sections and full-support, exactly.","rows":{"Z/25":{"all_orbits":"","antipodal_pairs":312,"full_support_orbits":"","sections":"","sl_order":15000},"Z/27":{"all_orbits":"","antipodal_pairs":364,"full_support_orbits":"","sections":"","sl_order":17496}}},"part_C_structural_formulas":{"identities":{"antipodal_pairs":"(p^{2n} - 1)/2","fix_all":"(p^n)^{c+(g)}","fix_full":"(p^n-1)^{c+(g)} if no negative cycle else 0","sections":"(p^n)^{(p^{2n}-1)/2}","sl_order":"|SL(2,Z/p^n)| = p^{3n-2} (p^2 - 1)"},"reading":"The group order, the antipodal-pair count and the section count are the stated closed forms, verified against the enumerated cells; only 2 being a unit enters the fixed-point counts, which is why the field and ring formulas coincide.","rows":{"Z/25":{"pairs":312,"pairs_formula":312,"sections_formula":"(25)^312","sl_order":15000,"sl_order_formula":15000},"Z/27":{"pairs":364,"pairs_formula":364,"sections_formula":"(27)^364","sl_order":17496,"sl_order_formula":17496},"Z/3":{"pairs":4,"pairs_formula":4,"sections_formula":"(3)^4","sl_order":24,"sl_order_formula":24},"Z/5":{"pairs":12,"pairs_formula":12,"sections_formula":"(5)^12","sl_order":120,"sl_order_formula":120},"Z/9":{"pairs":40,"pairs_formula":40,"sections_formula":"(9)^40","sl_order":648,"sl_order_formula":648}}},"schema":"w33.pass661.ring_burnside_closed_form.v1","status":"PASS"}
